For **Part II (Git & GitHub)**, you don't need to import anything into Eclipse specifically. First get the project on your computer, then perform the Git operations.

## Part II — Exact practical process

### Step 1: Get the project

If you haven't cloned it:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/deepthisagar7/library-management.git
```

Then:

```bash
cd library-management
```

---

## 2.a — Initialize and push

```bash
git init
git status
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/deepthisagar7/library-management.git
git push -u origin main
```

---

## 2.b — Change commit message

If the last commit says:

```text
Added Book Managment Module
```

change it to:

```text
Added Book Management Module
```

using:

```bash
git commit --amend -m "Added Book Management Module"
```

---

## 2.c — Create feature branch

```bash
git switch -c feature/book-management
```

---

## 2.d — Restore `Book.java`

If `Book.java` was accidentally deleted:

```bash
git restore Book.java
```

---

## 2.e — Merge conflict

```bash
git switch feature/book-management
git merge main
```

If there is a conflict:

```bash
git status
```

Open the conflicted file → resolve the code → remove:

```text
<<<<<<<
=======
>>>>>>>
```

Then:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict"
git push origin feature/book-management
```

---

## 2.f — Accidentally staged unwanted files

Check:

```bash
git status
```

Unstage everything:

```bash
git restore --staged .
```

Create `.gitignore`:

```gitignore
target/
*.class
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
.classpath
.project
.settings/
*.log
```

Then:

```bash
git add .gitignore
git add .
git status
```

---

## 2.g — Incorrect latest commit

### If NOT pushed:

```bash
git commit --amend
```

or:

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

### If ALREADY pushed:

```bash
git revert HEAD
git push
```

---

### So for Part II, the basic flow is:

```text
GitHub Repository
       ↓
git clone
       ↓
cd library-management
       ↓
git init
       ↓
git add .
       ↓
git commit
       ↓
git remote add origin
       ↓
git push
       ↓
branches / restore / merge conflicts / gitignore / revert
```

**You can do all of Part II from Git Bash/Terminal. Eclipse is not required for the Git commands**, except that you may use Eclipse to edit a file when resolving a merge conflict.
